"## Unicode Technical Standard 39 security: how skeleton algorithm and confusables data work, identifier restriction levels, detecting mixed-script spoofing with Greek Cyrillic homoglyphs, best practices for handling confusable characters in user identifiers\n# UTS #39 — Unicode Security Mechanisms: Research Summary\n\nUTS #39 defines mechanisms for detecting visually confusable strings (\"spoofing\") and restricting identifier content. It is updated with each annual Unicode release (most recently tracking Unicode 17.0, Sept 2025 — verify the current version). Note: I'm answering from canonical source knowledge; confirm details against the live spec.\n\n## 1. The Skeleton Algorithm and Confusables Data [1][2]\n\n**Skeleton computation** (UTS #39 §4). The skeleton of a string X is:\n\n1. Convert X to **NFD** (canonical decomposition) [9]\n2. Map each character through the **confusables mapping** (confusables.txt)\n3. Apply **CaseFolding** (full case folding)\n4. Convert to **NFD** again\n\nSo: `skeleton(X) = NFD(casefold(map(NFD(X))))`. Two identifiers are *confusable* if and only if their skeletons are identical.\n\n**confusables.txt** [2] format:\n```\n0441 ;  0063 ;  MA   # ( с → c ) CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER ES → LATIN SMALL LETTER C\n```\nMapping types:\n- **MA** — mixed-script confusable; **SA** — single-script confusable (apply regardless of case)\n- **SL** / **ML** — single/mixed-script confusables that apply only after lowercasing (relevant for case-insensitive matching; ICU's `ANY_CASE` option controls this) [7]\n\n**intentional.txt** [3] lists characters whose confusable mapping is deliberate (fullwidth forms, mathematical alphanumerics, compatibility characters). Data is glyph-shape based, reviewed across fonts, and revised annually — it is deliberately conservative and does **not** cover everything (e.g., `rn` ≈ `m`, font-specific shapes).\n\n## 2. Identifier Restriction Levels [1][4][5]\n\nImplemented via `Identifier_Status` (Allowed/Restricted) and `Identifier_Type` (Inclusion, Recommended, Technical, Exclusion, Not_XID, etc.) in DerivedCoreProperties.txt [4], plus the Recommended-script list in UAX #31 [5]:\n\n| Level | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Inclusion** | Only the small curated \"Inclusion\" set (essentially ASCII plus a few safe characters, e.g., middle dot) — highest security |\n| **Highly Restrictive** | Recommended scripts, but multiple scripts only within a *script group* ({Han, Bopomofo}, {Han, Hiragana, Katakana}, {Hangul}); each other script alone |\n| **Moderately Restrictive** | Latin may mix with other Recommended scripts **except Cyrillic, Greek, and Cherokee** (Cherokee's syllabary borrows Latin glyph shapes) |\n| **Minimally Restrictive** | Arbitrary mixing of Recommended scripts (e.g., Latin + Cyrillic allowed) |\n| **Unrestricted** | Any characters |\n\nScript mixing is evaluated using *augmented script sets* (ScriptExtensions.txt) [6] so Common characters (digits, punctuation) don't falsely trigger \"mixed-script.\"\n\n## 3. Detecting Mixed-Script Spoofing (Greek/Cyrillic Homoglyphs) [1][7]\n\n**Key homoglyph sets** (all mapped in confusables.txt):\n- Cyrillic → Latin: а→a, с→c, е→e, о→o, р→p, х→x, у→y, і→i, ѕ→s, ј→j, к→k, м→m, т→t\n- Greek → Latin: ο→o, α→a, ε→e, ι→i, κ→k, ν→v, ρ→p, τ→t, υ→u, χ→x\n- Cyrillic ↔ Greek directly: і (U+0456) ≈ ι (U+03B9), etc.\n\n**Detection procedure:**\n1. Normalize (NFKC_Casefold per UAX #31 identifier profile) [5][9]\n2. Resolve per-character script sets; if their intersection is empty → **mixed-script identifier**\n3. Compute the skeleton\n4. Classify:\n   - **Mixed-script confusable**: identifier is mixed-script but its skeleton is single-script (e.g., `аdmin` with Cyrillic а + Latin dmin → skeleton `admin` is pure Latin)\n   - **Whole-script confusable**: identifier is single-script but its skeleton is single-script in a *different* script (e.g., all-Cyrillic `соре` ≈ Latin `cope`)\n5. Pairwise: `skeleton(candidate) == skeleton(existing)` → block or verify\n\nICU's SpoofChecker exposes these as `MIXED_SCRIPT_CONFUSABLE`, `WHOLE_SCRIPT_CONFUSABLE`, `SINGLE_SCRIPT_CONFUSABLE`, plus `INVISIBLE` (zero-width/combining-mark tricks), `RESTRICTION_LEVEL`, and `CHAR_LIMIT` checks; `getSkeleton()` and `areConfusable()` are the core APIs [7]. ICU requires normalized input.\n\n**Worked example:** candidate `ρaypal` (Greek rho U+03C1). Scripts = {Greek, Latin} → mixed-script; skeleton = `paypal` → single-script → mixed-script confusable → reject, or flag because skeleton matches the existing account.\n\n## 4. Best Practices for User Identifiers [1][5][7][8]\n\n1. **Normalize canonically**: store and compare NFKC_Casefold forms; use full CaseFolding, not ASCII lowercase.\n2. **Enforce a restriction level** at creation: Highly Restrictive is a good default for global user-facing services; Inclusion/ASCII for high-assurance or monolingual services; Moderate for programming-language identifiers.\n3. **Skeleton-index check at registration**: persist `skeleton(username)` in an indexed column; on signup, compare candidate skeleton against all existing ones; on match, reject or require out-of-band verification (email/phone).\n4. **Reject invisible/spoofing characters**: default-ignorables, zero-width joiners, suspicious mark sequences (ICU `INVISIBLE`).\n5. **Keep data current**: confusables.txt changes every Unicode release; re-validate stored identifiers after upgrades.\n6. **Scope scripts to your audience**: allow Cyrillic/Greek for relevant locales, but never mixed with Latin under Highly/Moderately Restrictive.\n7. **UX mitigations**: warn users about mixed-script names at creation; annotate or inspect-render names in feeds; use fonts that differentiate homoglyphs; pair with verified badges and impersonation reporting.\n8. **Don't silently mutate** — reject with a clear error instead.\n9. **Know the limits**: skeleton matching is approximate and font-dependent; supplement with policy (UTR #36) [8], monitoring, and rate-limiting lookalike registrations.\n\n## Sources\n[1] UTS #39, *Unicode Security Mechanisms* — unicode.org/reports/tr39/\n[2] confusables.txt — unicode.org/Public/security/latest/confusables.txt\n[3] intentional.txt — unicode.org/Public/security/latest/intentional.txt\n[4] DerivedCoreProperties.txt (Identifier_Status/Identifier_Type) — unicode.org/Public/UCD/latest/ucd/DerivedCoreProperties.txt\n[5] UAX #31, *Identifier and Pattern Syntax* — unicode.org/reports/tr31/\n[6] UAX #24, *Script Property* / ScriptExtensions.txt — unicode.org/reports/tr24/\n[7] ICU Spoof Checker (ICU4C `uspoof` / ICU4J `SpoofChecker`) — unicode.org/reports/tr39/#ICU (userguide: strings/spoofchecker)\n[8] UTR #36, *Unicode Security Considerations* — unicode.org/reports/tr36/\n[9] UAX #15, *Normalization Forms* — unicode.org/reports/tr15/\n\n---\n\n## Detecting and mitigating invisible character and bidi spoofing attacks in text pipelines: Trojan Source attack, RLO/LRO overrides, UTS #55 source code handling, compiler warnings, GitHub invisible character warnings, OpenAI/LLM prompt injection via hidden Unicode characters\n# Invisible Character & Bidi Spoofing: Detection and Mitigation in Text Pipelines\n\n*Note: no live search tool was available for this query; the summary below reflects primary sources documented through early 2025, indexed at the end.*\n\n## 1. The Trojan Source attack\n\n- **Core mechanism:** Unicode bidirectional (bidi) control characters embedded in source code (comments, string literals, or identifiers) cause the *visual* rendering to differ from the *compiled/executed* semantics — e.g., an \"early return\" attack where malicious code appears to be inside a comment, or a string whose characters display in reversed order via RLO (U+202E) [1][2].\n- **Publication:** Boucher & Anderson (Cambridge), *\"Trojan Source: Invisible Vulnerabilities,\"* arXiv:2111.00169 (Nov 2021), later USENIX Security 2023 [1]. Coordinated disclosure to ~20 vendors; most patched within weeks [2].\n- **CVEs:** CVE-2021-42574 (bidi spoofing in source) and CVE-2021-42694 (homoglyph identifier spoofing, e.g., Cyrillic а U+0430 vs. Latin a) [3][4].\n- **Blast radius:** Nearly all major compilers/interpreters accepted bidi characters in comments and strings, making the attack language-agnostic (C/C++, Rust, Go, Swift, JavaScript, Python, etc.); the authors found bidi characters already present in real code in numerous popular repositories [1][2].\n\n## 2. Relevant invisible-character classes\n\n- **Bidi formatting:** LRE/RLE/PDF/LRO/RLO (U+202A–202E), isolates LRI/RLI/FSI/PDI (U+2066–2069), LRM/RLM (U+200E/F) [16].\n- **Zero-width/invisible:** ZWSP (U+200B), ZWNJ (U+200C), ZWJ (U+200D), WORD JOINER (U+2060), BOM/ZWNBSP (U+FEFF), soft hyphen (U+00AD).\n- **Tag characters:** U+E0000–U+E007F (legitimately used in emoji flag sequences, e.g., 🏴󠁧󠁢󠁳󠁣󠁴󠁿) — invisible in almost all renderers but machine-readable; central to LLM smuggling (§5).\n- **Homoglyphs:** cross-script confusables; handled via UTS #39 `confusables.txt` / skeleton algorithm and UAX #31 identifier profiles [6].\n\n## 3. UTS #55 and standards response\n\n- The Unicode Consortium published **UTS #55, \"Unicode Source Code Handling\"** (v1.0, late 2021) in direct response to Trojan Source. Recommendations: flag unpaired bidi controls in source; render bidi/invisible characters as visible escapes in editors, diffs, and terminals; restrict identifiers via UAX #31; guidance for code review tools and compilers [5].\n- Key nuance: outright banning bidi breaks legitimate RTL localization; UTS #55 favors *pairing* checks (stack-based, per UAX #9) and visibility over blanket prohibition [5][16].\n\n## 4. Compiler, editor, and hosting mitigations\n\n- **GCC 12:** new `-Wbidi-chars=[none|unpaired|any]` warning covering comments, string/char literals, and identifiers; enabled by default at the `unpaired` level [7].\n- **Rust 1.56:** lint `text_direction_codepoint_in_literal` (warn by default) flags bidi controls in literals [8].\n- **Clang/LLVM:** added diagnostics for unpaired bidi control characters in comments and literals (LLVM 14 era); LLVM also hardened its own review practices [9].\n- **VS Code 1.63 (Nov 2021):** default-on Unicode highlighting (`editor.unicodeHighlight.*`) for invisible and ambiguous/homoglyph characters, explicitly as a Trojan Source mitigation [11].\n- **GitHub:** yellow banner on files/commits containing bidirectional text — *\"This file contains bidirectional Unicode text that may be interpreted or compiled differently than what appears below\"* — plus a \"show hidden characters\" view (Oct–Nov 2021) [10]. GitLab rolled out comparable warnings [10].\n- **Important limits:** NFKC normalization does **not** remove zero-width or tag characters (they have no compatibility decompositions), and it does **not** unify cross-script homoglyphs (Cyrillic а and Latin a remain distinct) — so normalization alone is insufficient; filtering by code point/block and confusable detection are required [5][6].\n\n## 5. LLM prompt injection via hidden Unicode\n\n- **ASCII smuggling:** Tag characters (U+E0000–E007F) are invisible in chat UIs but are tokenized as ordinary byte-level tokens by LLM tokenizers. Demonstrated by Rehberger (\"ASCII smuggling,\" 2023–2024): hidden instructions ride into prompts, and model outputs can encode exfiltrated data invisibly — e.g., Microsoft Copilot appending tag-character-encoded data to URLs for exfiltration. Microsoft's MSRC addressed this in \"Securing AI Model Outputs\" (May 2024) [12].\n- **Hidden instructions:** Goodside (2023) demonstrated invisible prompt injection using Unicode tags with GPT models [13]; the academic paper *\"Invisible Prompt Injection: A Hijacking Attack on Large Language Model Applications\"* (arXiv, 2024) systematized hijacking via tag characters [14].\n- **Other vectors:** zero-width characters used to split keywords and evade safety filters; RLO/bidi characters in *model output* spoofing chat UIs, logs, and terminals; homoglyph substitution bypassing keyword filters. Indirect injection via hidden characters in RAG-retrieved documents/emails is a recognized subcase [12][14][15].\n- **Status:** No complete model-level fix exists; OWASP's LLM Top 10 lists prompt injection (LLM01) as the top risk, with input/output filtering as the primary mitigation [15].\n\n## 6. Practical detection/mitigation checklist for text pipelines\n\n1. **Scan** for bidi controls, zero-width, tag, and variation-selector characters, e.g. Python: `[\\u200b-\\u200f\\u202a-\\u202e\\u2060-\\u2064\\u2066-\\u2069\\u00ad\\ufeff\\U000e0000-\\U000e007f]` (plus FE00–FE0F/E0100–E01EF where relevant).\n2. **Escape or strip** rather than silently pass through; log raw code points. Preserve valid emoji flag tag sequences only if required.\n3. **Enforce bidi pairing** (stack-based per UAX #9) instead of blanket bans where RTL content is legitimate [5][16].\n4. **Homoglyph defense:** UTS #39 skeleton/confusables + single-script restriction for identifiers [6].\n5. **CI/CD:** enable GCC `-Wbidi-chars`, Rust's lint, Clang diagnostics; run the trojan-source detector tooling released with the paper [1][7][8][9].\n6. **LLM pipelines:** sanitize inputs *and* outputs in both directions; never render model output as raw HTML/Markdown; treat retrieved documents as untrusted [12][14][15].\n\n## References\n\n[1] Boucher & Anderson, \"Trojan Source: Invisible Vulnerabilities,\" arXiv:2111.00169 (2021); USENIX Security 2023.\n[2] trojansource.codes (project site: attack demos, vendor responses, detector tooling).\n[3] CVE-2021-42574 (bidi source-code spoofing).\n[4] CVE-2021-42694 (homoglyph identifier spoofing).\n[5] UTS #55, \"Unicode Source Code Handling,\" Unicode Consortium (2021–).\n[6] UAX #31 (Identifier and Pattern Syntax); UTS #39 (Unicode Security Mechanisms).\n[7] GCC 12 release notes / `-Wbidi-chars` documentation.\n[8] Rust 1.56.0 release notes (`text_direction_codepoint_in_literal`).\n[9] Clang/LLVM diagnostics for unpaired bidi control characters (LLVM 14 era).\n[10] GitHub Changelog (Oct–Nov 2021) bidirectional Unicode warnings; GitLab equivalent.\n[11] VS Code 1.63 release notes — Unicode highlighting.\n[12] Rehberger, Embrace The Red, \"ASCII Smuggling\" (2023–2024); Microsoft MSRC, \"Securing AI Model Outputs\" (2024).\n[13] Goodside, invisible prompt injection via Unicode tag characters (2023).\n[14] \"Invisible Prompt Injection: A Hijacking Attack on Large Language Model Applications\" (arXiv, 2024).\n[15] OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications — LLM01: Prompt Injection.\n[16] UAX #9, Unicode Bidirectional Algorithm."